!Proposed definitions

;__su'o__ (PA): At least (one).
;__su'e__ (PA): At most (one).
;__za'u__ (PA): More than (one).
;__me'i__ (PA): Less than (all).
;__ji'i__ (PA): Approximately.
;__ro__ (PA): Each. All.
;__da'a__ (PA): All except (one).
;__rau__ (PA): Enough. The right number.
;__du'e__ (PA): Too many. More than the right number.
;__mo'a__ (PA): Too few. Less than the right number.
;__no'o__ (PA): A vague number. Some number. A certain number.

!!Formal definitions

||

su'o PA | vei na'u zmadu ja dunli PA
su'e PA | vei na'u mleca ja dunli PA
za'u PA | vei na'u zmadu PA
me'i PA | vei na'u mleca PA
ji'i PA | vei na'u jibni PA

ro ''sumti'' | naku su'o ''sumti'' naku
da'a PA ''sumti'' | PA ''sumti'' naku

rau | vei ni'e drani
du'e | za'u rau
mo'a | me'i rau

no'o | vei ni'e namcu
||


!!Quantifiers

||
definite | :: no pa re ci vo mu xa ze bi ... da'aci da'are da'apa ro::
scalar | ::no [[...so'u...][[...so'o...][[...so'i...][[...so'e...][[...so'a...] ro::
generic | ::no [[..................... no'o .....................] ro::
subjective |  :: [[... mo'a ...] [[... rau ...] [[... du'e ...]::
||

||
__ranges__
su'o PA | ................. [[PA ...................]
za'u PA | ................. PA [[...................]
su'e PA | [[................ PA] ....................
me'i PA | [[................] PA ....................
ji'i PA | ............. [[... PA ...] ...............
||

||
__da'a__

da'a da'a PA | PA

da'a ro | no
da'a so'a | so'u
da'a so'e | so'o
da'a so'i | so'i
da'a so'o | so'e
da'a so'u | so'a
da'a no | ro

da'a rau | rau
da'a du'e | mo'a
da'a mo'a | du'e

da'a no'o | no'o

da'a su'o PA | su'e da'a PA
da'a su'e PA | su'o da'a PA
da'a za'u PA | me'i da'a PA
da'a me'i PA | za'u da'a PA
da'a ji'i PA | ji'i da'a PA
||

All definite quantifiers can be defined in terms of ''su'o'':

||
su'o N+1 da zo'u da broda | su'o da su'o N de zo'u ge da na du de gi da .e de broda 
za'u N da | su'o N+1 da
su'e N da | naku za'u N da
me'i N da | naku su'o N da
N da | su'o N da .e su'e N da
da'a Q da | Q da naku
|| 

In particular:

no da = me'ipa da = naku su'o da
ro da = da'ano da = no da naku = naku su'o da naku

All of the definitions hold for restricted da (i.e. da poi broda) as well. Then we have:

||
su'o da poi broda cu brode | su'o da zo'u da broda gi'e brode
su'o N da poi broda cu brode | su'o N da zo'u da broda gi'e brode
za'u N da poi broda cu brode | za'u N da zo'u da broda gi'e brode
su'e N da poi broda cu brode | su'e N da zo'u da broda gi'e brode
me'i N da poi broda cu brode | me'i N da zo'u da broda gi'e brode
N da poi broda cu brode | N da zo'u da broda gi'e brode
da'a Q da poi broda cu brode | da'a Q da zo'u da broda nagi'a brode
||



*The general form of a simple quantifier is:  

::__da'a range da'a (definite | indefinite) __::

where:

range = su'o | su'e | za'u | me'i | ji'i 
indefinite = ro | so'a | so'e | so'i | so'o | so'u | no | du'e | rau | mo'a | no'o 
definite = pa | re | ci | vo | mu | xa | ze | bi | so | pano | ... | ki'o | ... | ci'i

(no is among the indefinites just for convenience.)

Each component is optional. When the final component, definite or indefinite, is missing, a default value is understood: the default is ''pa'' in the case of ''su'o'', ''su'e'', ''za'u'' and ''da'a''; ''ro'' in the case of ''me'i''; and a vague number (perhaps ''no'o'') in the case of ''ji'i''.


*When two simple quantifiers as defined above are used one after the other, then if it gives no contradiction they are interpreted as if joined with ''.e'':

  ro ci broda 
  ''all three brodas, all brodas and three brodas.''

  rau su'o mu broda
  ''enough at least five brodas, enough brodas and at least five brodas.''

  su'o ci su'e bi broda
  ''at least three at most eight brodas''
  ''at least three brodas and at most eight brodas''
  ''between three and eight brodas (inclusive)''

*When two simple quantifiers are used one after the other, and joining them with ''.e'' would give a contradiction, they are interpreted as if joined with ''.a'':

  me'i ci za'u ci broda
  ''less than three more than three brodas''
  ''less than three brodas or more than three brodas''
  ''i.e. other than three brodas''

  mo'a du'e broda
  ''too few too many brodas''
  ''too few brodas or too many brodas''
  ''the wrong number of brodas''

!!! Quantifiers with ''fi'u''

The general form of a ''fi'u''-quantifier is:  

::__da'a range da'a definite fi'u definite __::

{Q1 ''fi'u'' Q2} is the proportional quantifier "Q1 out of every Q2":

re fi'u ci broda
''Two out of every three brodas.''
''Two thirds of brodas.''

za'u vo fi'u mu broda
''More than four out of every five brodas.''
''More than four fifths of brodas.''

Using indefinites with this format does not seem to make much sense.

!!! Quantifiers with ''fi'u ro''

The general form of a ''fi'u ro''-quantifier is:  

::__da'a range da'a (definite | indefinite) fi'u ro definite __::

{Q1 ''fi'u'' ro Q2} is "Q1 out of all Q2", i.e. it is equivalent to Q1, plus the specification of the value of "all".

du'a fi'u ro muno broda
''Too many out of all 50 broda.''
''Too many broda, of which there are 50 in all.''

!!! Quantifiers with ''pi'' and ''ce'i''

These have the general forms:

::__da'a range da'a pi definite __::

::__da'a range da'a definite pi definite ce'i __::

where {definite fi'u definite} has been replaced by its decimal or percentage equivalent. So {ci fi'u vo} is {pi zemu} or {zemu ce'i}.

 za'u pimu broda
 ''More than half of all brodas.''

 da'a cirepimu ce'i broda
 ''All but 32.5% of brodas.''

!!! Indefinite quantifiers with ''pi''

::__pi da'a range da'a indefinite __::

The effect is to make the quantifier non-distributive.

